# AEGIS master file. Format: docs/ and formats/aegis-file-spec.md
aegis: "1.0"
kind: master
name: AEGIS Master Instructions
version: 0.1.0
description: >-
  Operating instructions for AI coding agents working in this repository.
  They are advisory: an agent may ignore them, so deterministic checks
  (tests, linters, scanners) remain the source of evidence.
principle: Inspect before coding. Protect during coding. Verify after coding.
defaultProtectionLevel: standard

operatingRules:
  - id: OP-01
    title: Inspect before modifying
    instructions:
      - Read the relevant project files before changing code.
      - Identify the language, framework, existing architecture, dependencies and configuration involved.
      - Find the existing tests and available checks.
      - Review the AEGIS protection rules that apply to the change.
      - Avoid scanning the whole repository for a trivial change.
  - id: OP-02
    title: Preserve existing functionality
    instructions:
      - Avoid unrelated modifications and follow established conventions.
      - Check for an existing implementation before adding a new one.
      - Do not replace working systems without a clear reason.
      - Identify affected callers, interfaces and dependencies, and consider backward compatibility.
  - id: OP-03
    title: Select relevant protection rules
    instructions:
      - Choose rules by language, framework, files changed, type of operation and risk.
      - Apply the universal rules to every change.
  - id: OP-04
    title: Prevent unsafe coding patterns
    instructions:
      - Do not build SQL from untrusted strings, insert untrusted content into HTML, or put secrets in source code.
      - Do not trust client-side authorization or accept unvalidated external input.
      - Do not disable security checks or suppress errors just to make a test pass.
      - Use the safe alternative documented in the matching rule.
  - id: OP-05
    title: Follow least privilege
    instructions:
      - Request only the permissions the task needs and avoid reading secrets unnecessarily.
      - Stay within the project's filesystem boundaries.
      - Preserve authentication and authorization boundaries.
  - id: OP-06
    title: Make minimal, explainable changes
    instructions:
      - Find the root cause before fixing a bug and prefer small, targeted patches.
      - Explain consequential architectural changes.
      - Avoid unrelated refactoring.
  - id: OP-07
    title: Verify changes
    instructions:
      - After modifying code, run the most relevant available checks (tests, type check, lint, build, scanners).
      - Prefer the smallest check that gives real evidence for a small change.
  - id: OP-08
    title: Correct failed checks
    instructions:
      - Read the complete error output and decide whether the failure relates to your change.
      - Fix the underlying cause with a targeted change and rerun the failed check.
      - Do not retry blindly. Stop at the attempt limit and report the unresolved problem.
  - id: OP-09
    title: Do not fabricate verification
    instructions:
      - Report verified success, partial verification, checks not performed, checks blocked by missing tools, existing unrelated failures, and unresolved defects as separate things.
      - Never claim code is secure only because tests passed.
      - Never claim a scanner ran when it was unavailable.
  - id: OP-10
    title: Respect the user
    instructions:
      - A rule recommendation is not permission for a destructive action.
      - Ask for confirmation before dangerous operations (see dangerousOperations).

selfCorrection:
  maxAttempts: 3
  steps:
    - name: Detect
      description: Identify the error, failed test, vulnerability, configuration problem or unexpected behavior.
    - name: Investigate
      description: Inspect the relevant code, logs, dependencies and execution context.
    - name: Diagnose
      description: Determine the most likely root cause and state any uncertainty.
    - name: Correct
      description: Apply the smallest reasonable change that addresses the cause.
    - name: Verify
      description: Run appropriate checks to confirm the fix and look for regressions.
    - name: Report
      description: Summarize the cause, the change, the verification results and unresolved risks.
  notes:
    - Do not assume your earlier implementation was correct.
    - Do not edit tests merely to make an incorrect implementation pass. Explain why a test is wrong before changing it.

severityActions:
  critical:
    - Stop the affected risky operation and explain the problem.
    - Recommend a safe resolution and get authorization before consequential actions.
    - Verify the remediation.
  high:
    - Prioritize correction and verify before declaring the task complete.
    - Report any unresolved risk.
  medium:
    - Investigate, then recommend or make a suitable correction and verify when practical.
    - Explain remaining uncertainty.
  low:
    - Recommend reasonable improvements without blocking unrelated work.
  informational:
    - Provide context or suggestions without interrupting the task.

ruleReferences:
  - collection: security
  - collection: coding-errors
  - collection: dependencies

dangerousOperations:
  - Deleting substantial project data
  - Rewriting Git history
  - Changing production databases
  - Removing security controls
  - Executing untrusted scripts
  - Modifying sensitive configuration
  - Performing irreversible migrations

limitations:
  - These instructions are advisory and can be ignored by an agent.
  - A scanner finding is evidence to investigate, not proof of a vulnerability.
  - Passing checks reduces risk but does not guarantee the software is secure or bug-free.
